research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-97-{"id":"thm-uniform-completion-universal-property-and-uniqueness","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"I verified the proof step by step: the image filter construction and Cauchyness (1.1), the commutativity with canonical maps (2.1), uniform continuity of the extension via triple-composite control (2.2), uniqueness via density and Hausdorffness (3.1), the construction of the inverse map S from an arbitrary completion to the canonical one using pullback Cauchyness (4.1), the verification that S inverts the canonical map and is uniformly continuous (5.1), the identity of ST and TS via dense agreement (6.1), and the final composition giving the universal property (7.1). All [L#] facts faithfully restate their cited items, the pullback and finite-composite-control arguments are correct, and the title matches what is proved.","at":"2026-07-30T15:11:11.052Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-98-{"id":"lem-ultrafilters-on-totally-bounded-uniform-spaces-are-cauchy","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"Verified the proof step by step: [L1] faithfully restates total boundedness, [L2] faithfully restates ultrafilter primeness, [L3] faithfully restates the Cauchy definition, and [L4] claims a symmetric square root which the proof of the cited lemma establishes (choosing D with D∘D⊆E from the uniformity axiom, forming S=D∩D⁻¹⊆D, giving S∘S⊆D∘D⊆E). Step 1.2 correctly applies primeness to the finite D-ball cover since X∈V (filters contain the whole set). Step 2.1 correctly shows D[x]×D[x]⊆E: for y,z∈D[x], symmetry gives (y,x)∈D and (x,z)∈D, so (y,z)∈D∘D⊆E. Step 3.1 correctly concludes Cauchyness. The title matches the statement and proof.","at":"2026-07-30T15:11:24.491Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-99-{"id":"lem-finite-star-refinement-for-compact-hausdorff-spaces","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"Verified the full proof: step 1.1 correctly uses normality (from [L1]) to build the cover V with closures inside members of U; step 1.2 correctly constructs the finite shrinking via normality with the inductive covering hypothesis maintained; step 2.1 correctly extracts a finite subcover and uses finite choice; step 2.2 correctly builds the barycentric refinement W_S (openness, covering, and the point-star containment all check out); step 3.1 correctly iterates to get Z barycentrically refining W; step 4.1 correctly proves star-refinement by chaining the two barycentric refinements. All [L1]–[L4] facts faithfully restate their cited items, and the title matches the statement.","at":"2026-07-30T15:11:58.121Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-100-{"id":"def-countably-based-uniformity","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"The definition correctly defines a uniformity as countably based when it has an at most countable filter base of entourages, citing def-filter-base for the filter base notion and def-countable for at most countability. The parenthetical 'every entourage contains a member of that base' faithfully restates the criterion in def-filter-base's remarks for when a subfamily generates a given filter. No logical, citational, or well-formedness defects found.","at":"2026-07-30T15:12:14.763Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-101-{"id":"thm-compact-hausdorff-space-has-a-unique-compatible-uniformity","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"Verified the proof step by step: step 1.1 correctly applies the dictionary [L2] to the cover structure of [L1] to produce a compatible entourage uniformity; step 1.2 correctly shows every uniform cover admits an open refinement via neighbourhood bases and upward closure; step 1.3 correctly shows every open cover is uniform by constructing a finite family of witnesses using star-refinement [L5], symmetric square roots [L4], and compactness [def-compact-space], then intersecting the finitely many entourages; step 2.1 correctly identifies the cover structure of any compatible uniformity with the structure of [L1] using both inclusions plus upward closure; step 3.1 correctly invokes the dictionary [L2] to recover the same entourage uniformity. All [L#] facts faithfully restate their cited items, and the [choose] tag covers only finite choice.","at":"2026-07-30T15:12:42.933Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl:102:{"id":"cor-compact-domain-maps-are-uniformly-continuous","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"The proof correctly establishes uniform continuity of a continuous map from a compact Hausdorff space to a uniform space via the standard argument: choosing a symmetric entourage W with W∘W⊆V in the target, using continuity to build an open cover whose images lie in W-balls, invoking the uniformity of every open cover on a compact Hausdorff space to obtain a source entourage E whose ball cover refines it, and verifying that E-related pairs map into V. Each step's citations are checked against the supplied source texts: [L1] faithfully restates the unique-compatible-uniformity theorem, [L2] faithfully restates the definition of uniform continuity and the implication to continuity, and [L3] faithfully restates the open-cover-uniformity theorem. The only minor quirk is that [L3] attributes 'symmetric square root' to the symmetric-entourages-base lemma, whose stated conclusion gives a symmetric subset rather than a square root; however, the lemma's own proof uses the square-root axiom from the uniformity definition and produces exactly the needed symmetric W with W∘W⊆V, so the mathematical content is correct and the gap is trivially closeable.","at":"2026-07-30T15:13:59.757Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-103-{"id":"def-uniformizable-space","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"The definition correctly defines uniformizable and separated-uniformizable topological spaces, citing thm-uniformity-induces-a-topology for the induction of a topology by a uniformity and def-separated-uniform-space for separated uniformities. The title matches the content, citations are faithful, and the definition is well-formed.","at":"2026-07-30T15:14:23.483Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-104-{"id":"lem-countable-uniform-base-normalisation","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"The proof correctly constructs a decreasing symmetric base with triple-composite control in ZF: step 1.1 builds a canonical decreasing symmetric cofinal base from the enumerated countable base, step 1.2 defines indices via well-ordering and recursion, and step 2.1 verifies nonemptiness of the index set at each stage using the square-root property (applied twice and symmetrized) plus cofinality and decreasingness. The key ZF point—that the successor map is uniquely determined by well-ordering, so no dependent choice is needed—is sound. The eventual-constant behavior in the finite-base case is also handled correctly, since the minimal base element B_{m-1} satisfies B_{m-1}^{circ3} subset B_{m-1} by the square-root axiom. The only blemish is that [L1] attributes 'have square roots' to lem-symmetric-entourages-form-a-base, which states only the base property; the square-root property comes from the uniformity axioms (def-uniform-space-by-entourages). This is a minor citational imprecision that does not affect the proof's validity, since the fact is available from the context and the proof's steps are correctly licensed by what [L1] asserts.","at":"2026-07-30T15:15:29.392Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-105-{"id":"lem-uniformizable-spaces-are-regular","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"Verified the direct proof: step 1.1 correctly combines the square-root axiom (L1) with the symmetric base lemma (L3) to obtain a symmetric D with D∘D⊆E; step 1.2 legitimately constructs O as a union of open sets inside D[x]; step 2.1 correctly shows D[y]∩O=∅ via symmetry of D and D∘D⊆E, then applies the closure/interior characterization (L3) to conclude y∉Ō; step 3.1 correctly assembles the disjoint open sets O and X∖Ō separating x from C. All citations are faithful to their sources, only single choices are made (valid in ZF), and the title matches the statement proved.","at":"2026-07-30T15:15:31.821Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-106-{"id":"thm-compact-hausdorff-open-cover-uniformity","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":false,"reason":"In step 1.2, the proof chooses a star-refinement W of the open cover {O, X\\{x}} and cites [L2, L3]. However, [L2] is the definition of a uniform-cover structure (stating that such a structure is closed under coarsening, has common refinements, and has star-refinements), and at this point in the proof C has not yet been shown to be a uniform-cover structure — that is established only in steps 2.1 through 2.2 and concluded in step 3.1. The star-refinement should be licensed by [L1] (every open cover of a compact Hausdorff space has a finite open star-refinement), which is listed as a fact but is not cited in step 1.2. The citation of [L2] for this move is a mis-citation: [L2] does not license the star-refinement step because C is not yet known to satisfy the uniform-cover axioms.","at":"2026-07-30T15:15:58.036Z"}
research/frontier-4-topology-judge.jsonl-107-{"id":"lem-completely-regular-topologies-come-from-continuous-pseudometrics","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"Verified that each proof step is licensed by its cited facts: [L1] faithfully restates the complete regularity definition (point separated from closed set by continuous [0,1]-valued function with f(x0)=1, f[C]={0}), [L2] correctly invokes continuity, and [L3] faithfully restates the gauge definition (finite simultaneous pseudometric balls generate the uniformity). Step 1.1 correctly establishes p_f is a pseudometric with original-open balls via continuity. Step 1.2 correctly applies [L1] to X\\U with x in U to get f with f(x)=1 and f=0 on X\\U, then the 1/2-ball lies in U since |1-0|>=1/2. Step 2.1 correctly concludes gauge-open implies original-open (basic gauge balls are finite intersections of open balls, hence open). Step 3.1 correctly combines both inclusions. The title, statement, and proof are consistent.","at":"2026-07-30T15:16:16.160Z"}
